  "preamble": "The voiceless dental affricate [ts] of medieval Ibero-Romance and northern Italo-Romance became the interdental fricative [θ] in several varieties. The change is indirect: the affricate first lost its stop phase, giving a dental sibilant [s̪], and this sibilant was then fronted to a non-sibilant interdental [θ]. Castilian Spanish is the classic case, and Galician and conservative Venetan dialects (Bellunese, north Trevisan) did the same independently. In Albanian, the Proto-Albanian affricate *ts (mostly from PIE *ḱ) became th [θ] (*tsurja > thur 'to plait').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Deaffrication, Interdentalization, Loss of Sibilance",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Spanish (Castilian)",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Spanish ⟨ç⟩, ⟨c⟩ before e/i = [t͡s̪] (caça 'hunt', cinco 'five')",
          "to": "caza [ˈkaθa], cinco [ˈθiŋko]",
          "note": "By 1600 /t͡s̪/ had deaffricated and merged with the /s̪/ from devoiced /d͡z̪/. This dental sibilant was later fronted to interdental /θ/, the basis of modern distinción."
        }
      ]
